{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey Anti-Eviction Act good-cause eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may end a residential tenancy only for a statutory good cause, such as nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct or lease violations, after serving the required Notice to Quit; some causes require relocation assistance before eviction.","key_value":"Notice to Quit: 3 days to 3 years depending on cause","coverage_conditions":"Most residential rentals: single-family homes, mobile homes and mobile home park land, apartment buildings, complexes, and rooming and boarding homes.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":true,"other":"Single-family homes, mobile homes, apartment buildings, complexes, rooming and boarding homes"},"exemptions":"Two- or three-unit owner-occupied premises with two or fewer rental units; hotel, motel or guest house transient and seasonal guests (continuous guests with no alternate residence are covered); a unit held in trust for a permanently occupying immediate family member with developmental disabilities.","penalty":"For illegal-occupancy evictions relocation assistance equals six times the monthly rent; if unpaid, 18% annual interest accrues and the amount due is a lien on the property.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"A Notice to Quit must be served on the tenant at least three months before filing a suit for eviction. The tenant can’t be evicted until relocation assistance is provided.","interaction":"A rent-increase eviction ground requires the increase to comply with rent control and other municipal ordinances.","confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-03","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"New Jersey ban on self-help evictions and lockouts","requirement":"A landlord, or anyone acting for the landlord, may not evict or lock out a tenant, shut off utilities or block entry without a court judgment; only a court officer may physically evict after a warrant is issued.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Applies to all residential dwelling units in New Jersey and to landlords, their agents and others acting on the landlord's behalf.","applicability":{"built_on_or_before":null,"built_after":null,"date_basis":null,"min_units":null,"max_units":null,"owner_dependent":false,"other":"All residential dwelling units"},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"A landlord who unlawfully enters or holds a tenant's belongings by force or threat may be liable for damages; a successful forcible entry and detainer action may award possession and damages including court costs and reasonable attorney fees; the landlord may be charged with disorderly conduct.","effective_date":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:39-1","quoted_span":"Self-help evictions, or lockouts, made by the landlord are illegal in New Jersey.","interaction":null,"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
